00:34:33,280 --> 00:34:38,320
So, from this point onward, we'll move

913
00:34:35,280 --> 00:34:40,800
from the hardware foundation of MRI into

914
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:42,800
the timing and control of signals, which

915
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:45,359
is where much of the image contrast and

916
00:34:42,800 --> 00:34:48,359
diagnostic power of MRI truly comes

917
00:34:45,359 --> 00:34:48,359
from.

918
00:34:49,679 --> 00:34:53,760
Now we're entering one of the most

919
00:34:51,359 --> 00:34:56,639
important ideas in MRI phase and

920
00:34:53,760 --> 00:34:58,880
frequency encoding. Essentially MRI

921
00:34:56,639 --> 00:35:00,560
operates in what we call a forier

922
00:34:58,880 --> 00:35:03,119
imaging mode. So understanding the

923
00:35:00,560 --> 00:35:06,560
forier transform is absolutely critical

924
00:35:03,119 --> 00:35:08,079
to understanding how images are formed.

925
00:35:06,560 --> 00:35:10,000
This slide is really the key to

926
00:35:08,079 --> 00:35:11,520
everything that follows. If you can

927
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:13,520
grasp what's happening here, all the

928
00:35:11,520 --> 00:35:15,440
other pulse sequences, no matter how

929
00:35:13,520 --> 00:35:17,200
complicated they look, will make much

930
00:35:15,440 --> 00:35:19,119
more sense.

931
00:35:17,200 --> 00:35:20,960
In the stationary reference frame, the

932
00:35:19,119 --> 00:35:23,839
overall signal that we detect, or

933
00:35:20,960 --> 00:35:25,920
equivalently, the magnetization vector M

934
00:35:23,839 --> 00:35:29,040
is proportional to the proton density

935
00:35:25,920 --> 00:35:31,359
written as our row of XY. This function

936
00:35:29,040 --> 00:35:34,000
describes how many protons exist at each

937
00:35:31,359 --> 00:35:35,839
point in a given slice.

938
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:37,680
When we apply phase encoding and

939
00:35:35,839 --> 00:35:40,240
frequency encoding gradients, we

940
00:35:37,680 --> 00:35:42,640
introduce exponential phase factors into

941
00:35:40,240 --> 00:35:45,040
the signal. Mathematically, this means

942
00:35:42,640 --> 00:35:51,200
we multiply the signal by e to the power

943
00:35:45,040 --> 00:35:56,640
of j * gamma * gx * x * t and also by e

944
00:35:51,200 --> 00:35:59,440
to the power of -j * gamma * g y * y * p

945
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:02,000
where gamma is the gyromagnetic ratio.

946
00:35:59,440 --> 00:36:04,400
gx and gy are the gradient field

947
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:07,359
strengths and t and tao pi are the times

948
00:36:04,400 --> 00:36:09,440
for which those gradients are applied.

949
00:36:07,359 --> 00:36:11,359
These exponential terms introduce both

950
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:13,839
phase and frequency variations across

951
00:36:11,359 --> 00:36:15,520
the image slice. The phase encoding adds

952
00:36:13,839 --> 00:36:17,520
a phase shift that depends on position

953
00:36:15,520 --> 00:36:19,200
in the y direction and the frequency

954
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:21,760
encoding controls how the signal

955
00:36:19,200 --> 00:36:23,760
oscillates in time for each x position.

956
00:36:21,760 --> 00:36:26,000
Together these variations give each

957
00:36:23,760 --> 00:36:28,960
location, each small voxil a unique

958
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:30,800
combination of frequency and phase.

959
00:36:28,960 --> 00:36:32,800
That's what allows us to reconstruct a

960
00:36:30,800 --> 00:36:34,800
tomographic image. Without this

961
00:36:32,800 --> 00:36:36,720
encoding, all the information from the

962
00:36:34,800 --> 00:36:39,520
slice would be averaged together and

963
00:36:36,720 --> 00:36:41,680
we'd lose spatial detail.

964
00:36:39,520 --> 00:36:43,520
Now, by performing a few mathematical

965
00:36:41,680 --> 00:36:46,079
steps, specifically dividing and

966
00:36:43,520 --> 00:36:47,920
multiplying by 2p, we can rewrite these

967
00:36:46,079 --> 00:36:50,480
expressions in a form that matches the

968
00:36:47,920 --> 00:36:55,440
forier transform. We define two

969
00:36:50,480 --> 00:37:01,359
variables kx= gamma * gx * t / 2 pi and

970
00:36:55,440 --> 00:37:04,640
ky = gamma * gy * ti / 2 pi. Using these

971
00:37:01,359 --> 00:37:06,880
definitions, the detected signal s of kx

972
00:37:04,640 --> 00:37:10,000
ky becomes proportional to the double

973
00:37:06,880 --> 00:37:15,359
integral of rho of x y * e to the power

974
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:17,839
of j2 pi * kx * x + ky * y integrated

975
00:37:15,359 --> 00:37:20,000
over x and y. This is exactly the

976
00:37:17,839 --> 00:37:23,440
two-dimensional forier transform of the

977
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:25,520
object function ro of x y.

978
00:37:23,440 --> 00:37:28,240
So the measured data that we collect in

979
00:37:25,520 --> 00:37:30,800
MRI what we call kspace is simply the

980
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:33,440
forier transform of the spatial proton

981
00:37:30,800 --> 00:37:35,359
density. Once we fill kspace by varying

982
00:37:33,440 --> 00:37:37,520
the gradient fields we apply an inverse

983
00:37:35,359 --> 00:37:39,920
fier transform to reconstruct the image

984
00:37:37,520 --> 00:37:42,079
in real space.

985
00:37:39,920 --> 00:37:44,000
Now in practice the process can go in

986
00:37:42,079 --> 00:37:46,079
either order. You can perform phase

987
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:48,800
encoding first followed by frequency

988
00:37:46,079 --> 00:37:50,960
encoding or vice versa. For each phase

989
00:37:48,800 --> 00:37:53,200
encoding step, we record one line of

990
00:37:50,960 --> 00:37:55,280
data in kspace. And by repeating this

991
00:37:53,200 --> 00:37:57,760
process many times, we gradually fill

992
00:37:55,280 --> 00:37:59,520
the entire kspace grid. The green

993
00:37:57,760 --> 00:38:01,520
trajectory shown on the slide

994
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:03,440
illustrates how we move through kspace.

995
00:38:01,520 --> 00:38:05,520
Phase encoding determines the starting

996
00:38:03,440 --> 00:38:07,040
position and frequency encoding defines

997
00:38:05,520 --> 00:38:08,720
the direction in which data are

998
00:38:07,040 --> 00:38:10,720
collected.

999
00:38:08,720 --> 00:38:13,359
Different pulse sequences change the way

1000
00:38:10,720 --> 00:38:15,680
we traverse kspace. sometimes line by

1001
00:38:13,359 --> 00:38:17,520
line, sometimes in spirals or zigzags,

1002
00:38:15,680 --> 00:38:19,760
but the underlying principle is always

1003
00:38:17,520 --> 00:38:21,760
the same. Finally, because of symmetry,

1004
00:38:19,760 --> 00:38:24,000
if the image function is purely real, we

1005
00:38:21,760 --> 00:38:26,079
theoretically need to sample only half

1006
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:28,240
of Kspace.

1007
00:38:26,079 --> 00:38:30,560
However, in practice, we usually sample

1008
00:38:28,240 --> 00:38:33,359
the full space to ensure higher accuracy

1009
00:38:30,560 --> 00:38:35,440
and image stability. So, to summarize,

1010
00:38:33,359 --> 00:38:37,119
phase encoding and frequency encoding

1011
00:38:35,440 --> 00:38:39,760
translate spatial information into

1012
00:38:37,119 --> 00:38:41,920
distinct frequency and phase variations.

1013
00:38:39,760 --> 00:38:45,040
The signals we collect form the fier

1014
00:38:41,920 --> 00:38:47,440
representation of the object in kspace.

1015
00:38:45,040 --> 00:38:49,599
By applying the inverse fier transform,

1016
00:38:47,440 --> 00:38:52,320
we convert this frequency domain data

1017
00:38:49,599 --> 00:38:54,400
back into a real tomographic image. This

1018
00:38:52,320 --> 00:38:56,240
is the essence of the kspace theorem and

1019
00:38:54,400 --> 00:39:01,000
it's the mathematical foundation of

1020
00:38:56,240 --> 00:39:01,000
nearly all MRI imaging methods.

1021
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:07,280
Now that we understand how MRI encoding

1022
00:39:04,960 --> 00:39:09,200
works through Forier principles, let's

1023
00:39:07,280 --> 00:39:11,599
see how this idea is applied in one of

1024
00:39:09,200 --> 00:39:14,880
the most fundamental pulse sequences,

1025
00:39:11,599 --> 00:39:17,200
the spin echo imaging sequence.

1026
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:19,040
In this diagram, the horizontal axis

1027
00:39:17,200 --> 00:39:21,359
represents time and you can see the

1028
00:39:19,040 --> 00:39:23,280
major events in the sequence. the radio

1029
00:39:21,359 --> 00:39:25,520
frequency pulses, the gradients for

1030
00:39:23,280 --> 00:39:27,680
slice selection, phase encoding and

1031
00:39:25,520 --> 00:39:29,920
frequency encoding, and finally the

1032
00:39:27,680 --> 00:39:33,119
signal readout by the analog to digital

1033
00:39:29,920 --> 00:39:35,200
converter. The process begins with a 90°

1034
00:39:33,119 --> 00:39:38,320
radio frequency pulse which flips the

1035
00:39:35,200 --> 00:39:40,960
magnetization vector M from the Zaxis

1036
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:42,800
into the XY plane.

1037
00:39:40,960 --> 00:39:44,880
Immediately after this, the spins start

1038
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:47,440
to defphase because of local field in

1039
00:39:44,880 --> 00:39:49,760
homogeneities. Some spins precess a bit

1040
00:39:47,440 --> 00:39:51,760
faster, others a bit slower. As a

1041
00:39:49,760 --> 00:39:53,920
result, the transverse magnetization

1042
00:39:51,760 --> 00:39:56,800
gradually fades away. To correct this

1043
00:39:53,920 --> 00:39:58,560
defasing, a 180°ree pulse is applied

1044
00:39:56,800 --> 00:40:01,839
halfway through the time interval known

1045
00:39:58,560 --> 00:40:03,599
as TE or echo time.

1046
00:40:01,839 --> 00:40:05,680
This pulse effectively flips all the

1047
00:40:03,599 --> 00:40:07,599
spins. The ones that were ahead now fall

1048
00:40:05,680 --> 00:40:09,760
behind and those that were behind move

1049
00:40:07,599 --> 00:40:11,520
ahead. After an equal amount of time,

1050
00:40:09,760 --> 00:40:14,160
all the spins come back into phase and

1051
00:40:11,520 --> 00:40:16,400
an echo signal is formed.

1052
00:40:14,160 --> 00:40:18,400
That's why we call it a spin echo. The

1053
00:40:16,400 --> 00:40:21,280
system rephases itself to produce a

1054
00:40:18,400 --> 00:40:23,119
measurable signal. The TR or repetition

1055
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:26,079
time is the total time between

1056
00:40:23,119 --> 00:40:28,640
successive 90° pulses.

1057
00:40:26,079 --> 00:40:30,480
By changing TE and TR, we can emphasize

1058
00:40:28,640 --> 00:40:33,119
different types of image contrast. For

1059
00:40:30,480 --> 00:40:35,599
example, T1 waiting, T2 waiting, or

1060
00:40:33,119 --> 00:40:37,200
proton density waiting.

1061
00:40:35,599 --> 00:40:39,119
Now, if you look at the lower part of

1062
00:40:37,200 --> 00:40:41,680
the diagram, you'll see three gradient

1063
00:40:39,119 --> 00:40:44,320
waveforms. G slice selects the imaging

1064
00:40:41,680 --> 00:40:46,560
slice. G phase applies a brief pulse to

1065
00:40:44,320 --> 00:40:49,359
encode phase differences along the Y

1066
00:40:46,560 --> 00:40:51,359
direction and G fck or the frequency

1067
00:40:49,359 --> 00:40:53,520
encoding gradient is turned on during

1068
00:40:51,359 --> 00:40:56,480
the echo readout to separate spatial

1069
00:40:53,520 --> 00:40:58,640
information along the X direction.

1070
00:40:56,480 --> 00:41:00,560
The signal is then digitized by the AD

1071
00:40:58,640 --> 00:41:03,040
converter forming one line of data in

1072
00:41:00,560 --> 00:41:04,960
Kspace. By repeating the process with

1073
00:41:03,040 --> 00:41:07,440
different phase encoding gradients, we

1074
00:41:04,960 --> 00:41:09,520
fill up all the lines of kspace. After

1075
00:41:07,440 --> 00:41:12,480
that an inverse forier transform

1076
00:41:09,520 --> 00:41:14,480
converts the data into the final image.

1077
00:41:12,480 --> 00:41:16,480
So in summary, the spin echo sequence

1078
00:41:14,480 --> 00:41:19,119
uses a 90deree pulse to excite the

1079
00:41:16,480 --> 00:41:20,960
spins, a 180°ree pulse to rephase them,

1080
00:41:19,119 --> 00:41:22,960
and a carefully timed set of gradients

1081
00:41:20,960 --> 00:41:25,520
to encode spatial information in both

1082
00:41:22,960 --> 00:41:27,520
frequency and phase.

1083
00:41:25,520 --> 00:41:30,000
This sequence forms the foundation of

1084
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:32,480
most MRI imaging techniques and every

1085
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:34,800
other sequence including gradient echo,

1086
00:41:32,480 --> 00:41:36,800
echoplaner, and spiral imaging can be

1087
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:39,800
understood as a variation of this same

1088
00:41:36,800 --> 00:41:39,800
principle.

1089
00:41:41,280 --> 00:41:46,160
Now let's look at how multi-slice

1090
00:41:43,040 --> 00:41:48,240
imaging works in MRI. In MRI, slice

1091
00:41:46,160 --> 00:41:50,079
selection allows us to excite only a

1092
00:41:48,240 --> 00:41:52,560
specific region of the body instead of

1093
00:41:50,079 --> 00:41:54,640
the entire volume. This is achieved by

1094
00:41:52,560 --> 00:41:56,720
manipulating the resonance frequency of

1095
00:41:54,640 --> 00:41:58,800
spins within a narrow slice using a

1096
00:41:56,720 --> 00:42:00,720
gradient field.

1097
00:41:58,800 --> 00:42:02,960
When we apply the gradient, the magnetic

1098
00:42:00,720 --> 00:42:05,200
field strength changes linearly along

1099
00:42:02,960 --> 00:42:07,200
the gradient direction. As a result,

1100
00:42:05,200 --> 00:42:10,160
spins in different positions experience

1101
00:42:07,200 --> 00:42:12,400
slightly different lore frequencies.

1102
00:42:10,160 --> 00:42:14,319
By sending in a radio frequency pulse

1103
00:42:12,400 --> 00:42:16,079
that matches the frequency range of one

1104
00:42:14,319 --> 00:42:18,880
specific slice, we can flip the

1105
00:42:16,079 --> 00:42:20,960
magnetization vector M only within that

1106
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:23,359
selected slice.

1107
00:42:20,960 --> 00:42:24,800
The rest of the body remains unaffected.

1108
00:42:23,359 --> 00:42:26,720
However, because the field varies

1109
00:42:24,800 --> 00:42:28,800
linearly across the slice, the spins

1110
00:42:26,720 --> 00:42:30,480
acquire different phase shifts. Spins in

1111
00:42:28,800 --> 00:42:32,640
regions of stronger field accumulate

1112
00:42:30,480 --> 00:42:34,800
larger phase factors and those in weaker

1113
00:42:32,640 --> 00:42:37,680
fields accumulate smaller ones. This

1114
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:39,280
variation is called defasing.

1115
00:42:37,680 --> 00:42:41,520
To correct for this, we apply a

1116
00:42:39,280 --> 00:42:43,839
refocusing or rephasing pulse that has

1117
00:42:41,520 --> 00:42:46,560
the opposite polarity. This pulse

1118
00:42:43,839 --> 00:42:48,160
restores the overall phase coherence. It

1119
00:42:46,560 --> 00:42:50,560
doesn't bring every spin perfectly back

1120
00:42:48,160 --> 00:42:52,319
to its original state, but rephases them

1121
00:42:50,560 --> 00:42:54,480
on average so that the net signal

1122
00:42:52,319 --> 00:42:56,400
becomes measurable again. In the context

1123
00:42:54,480 --> 00:42:59,440
of slice selection, we call this process

1124
00:42:56,400 --> 00:43:01,920
defocusing and refocusing.

1125
00:42:59,440 --> 00:43:03,839
The refocusing gradient usually has half

1126
00:43:01,920 --> 00:43:05,680
the area of the initial defocusing

1127
00:43:03,839 --> 00:43:07,920
gradient because we only need to correct

1128
00:43:05,680 --> 00:43:10,560
the average phase dispersion, not the

1129
00:43:07,920 --> 00:43:12,240
exact microscopic alignment.

1130
00:43:10,560 --> 00:43:14,480
A similar process occurs during

1131
00:43:12,240 --> 00:43:16,480
frequency encoding. When the frequency

1132
00:43:14,480 --> 00:43:19,040
encoding gradient is applied, it

1133
00:43:16,480 --> 00:43:20,960
introduces another phase variation. To

1134
00:43:19,040 --> 00:43:22,720
recover the maximum signal strength, a

1135
00:43:20,960 --> 00:43:24,480
negative gradient pulse with half the

1136
00:43:22,720 --> 00:43:27,520
area of the first one is applied to

1137
00:43:24,480 --> 00:43:29,599
refocus the spins again. Although the

1138
00:43:27,520 --> 00:43:33,040
polarity of the gradient may appear the

1139
00:43:29,599 --> 00:43:35,040
same in diagrams, the 180° pulse inverts

1140
00:43:33,040 --> 00:43:36,960
the spin directions, effectively

1141
00:43:35,040 --> 00:43:38,960
flipping the polarity of the phase so

1142
00:43:36,960 --> 00:43:41,040
that the final echo appears consistent

1143
00:43:38,960 --> 00:43:42,880
in direction.

1144
00:43:41,040 --> 00:43:44,720
The phase encoding gradient then

1145
00:43:42,880 --> 00:43:46,880
introduces controlled phase shifts

1146
00:43:44,720 --> 00:43:49,200
across the slice, allowing each spatial

1147
00:43:46,880 --> 00:43:51,520
location to contribute a unique signal

1148
00:43:49,200 --> 00:43:53,680
component. These differences are what

1149
00:43:51,520 --> 00:43:55,920
allow us to reconstruct distinct pixel

1150
00:43:53,680 --> 00:43:57,760
values in the final image. In the spin

1151
00:43:55,920 --> 00:44:00,240
echo sequence, the two key timing

1152
00:43:57,760 --> 00:44:03,040
parameters are the echo time TE and the

1153
00:44:00,240 --> 00:44:05,040
repetition time TR.

1154
00:44:03,040 --> 00:44:07,119
By carefully adjusting these parameters,

1155
00:44:05,040 --> 00:44:09,280
we can control image contrast. And

1156
00:44:07,119 --> 00:44:11,119
importantly, the time between echoes can

1157
00:44:09,280 --> 00:44:13,280
also be used for additional slice

1158
00:44:11,119 --> 00:44:15,440
selection. That's how we can acquire

1159
00:44:13,280 --> 00:44:18,800
multiple slices within a single TR

1160
00:44:15,440 --> 00:44:20,880
period. As you see in the example here,

1161
00:44:18,800 --> 00:44:22,960
each slice is excited and read out in

1162
00:44:20,880 --> 00:44:26,160
sequence using its own frequency offset

1163
00:44:22,960 --> 00:44:28,240
labeled as omega 1, omega 2, omega3, and

1164
00:44:26,160 --> 00:44:30,400
so on. This is called multi slice

1165
00:44:28,240 --> 00:44:32,000
imaging. It's a highly efficient way to

1166
00:44:30,400 --> 00:44:36,760
cover a larger region of the body

1167
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:36,760
without increasing the total scan time.

1168
00:44:37,760 --> 00:44:42,160
In multi-lice imaging, we can further

1169
00:44:39,760 --> 00:44:44,160
optimize how slices are acquired. For

1170
00:44:42,160 --> 00:44:45,920
example, we can excite slice three, then

1171
00:44:44,160 --> 00:44:48,000
slice four, and then slice two,

1172
00:44:45,920 --> 00:44:49,680
alternating the order to maximize time

1173
00:44:48,000 --> 00:44:51,760
efficiency.

1174
00:44:49,680 --> 00:44:53,599
This alternating strategy helps reduce

1175
00:44:51,760 --> 00:44:55,520
the blurring or cross talk that can

1176
00:44:53,599 --> 00:44:57,920
occur when slices are too close together

1177
00:44:55,520 --> 00:44:59,520
in time or space. By allowing enough

1178
00:44:57,920 --> 00:45:01,920
separation between successive

1179
00:44:59,520 --> 00:45:04,000
exitations, we achieve cleaner and more

1180
00:45:01,920 --> 00:45:05,680
distinct images.

1181
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:07,520
Now, the spin echo sequence we just

1182
00:45:05,680 --> 00:45:09,520
discussed provides an excellent signal

1183
00:45:07,520 --> 00:45:12,079
to noise ratio because it effectively

1184
00:45:09,520 --> 00:45:14,720
cancels out the defasing caused by T2

1185
00:45:12,079 --> 00:45:16,560
star effects. This gives us a pure T2-

1186
00:45:14,720 --> 00:45:18,880
weighted signal that's highly reliable

1187
00:45:16,560 --> 00:45:20,960
for tissue contrast. However, the main

1188
00:45:18,880 --> 00:45:24,079
drawback is that the spin echo sequence

1189
00:45:20,960 --> 00:45:26,160
takes a long time. A full imaging

1190
00:45:24,079 --> 00:45:28,000
session can easily last half an hour or

1191
00:45:26,160 --> 00:45:30,240
more, which is often too slow for many

1192
00:45:28,000 --> 00:45:32,560
clinical applications. To address this

1193
00:45:30,240 --> 00:45:34,800
limitation, we use gradient echo imaging

1194
00:45:32,560 --> 00:45:37,760
as an alternative.

1195
00:45:34,800 --> 00:45:40,560
In a gradient echo sequence, there is no

1196
00:45:37,760 --> 00:45:42,720
180° refocusing pulse. This emission

1197
00:45:40,560 --> 00:45:46,160
greatly shortens both the echo time or

1198
00:45:42,720 --> 00:45:48,560
TE and the repetition time TR. Because

1199
00:45:46,160 --> 00:45:50,880
there's no refocusing pulse, gradient

1200
00:45:48,560 --> 00:45:53,280
echo imaging does not compensate for

1201
00:45:50,880 --> 00:45:55,760
magnetic field inhomogeneities. So the

1202
00:45:53,280 --> 00:46:00,560
signal decays more quickly according to

1203
00:45:55,760 --> 00:46:02,560
T2 star relaxation rather than pure T2.

1204
00:46:00,560 --> 00:46:05,599
To keep the signal strong and the scan

1205
00:46:02,560 --> 00:46:07,359
fast, the echo time TE is kept as short

1206
00:46:05,599 --> 00:46:09,520
as possible, typically just a few

1207
00:46:07,359 --> 00:46:11,359
milliseconds. In addition, the flip

1208
00:46:09,520 --> 00:46:13,280
angle of the radio frequency pulse is

1209
00:46:11,359 --> 00:46:16,079
reduced to a small value, usually well

1210
00:46:13,280 --> 00:46:18,400
below 90°. This allows for rapid

1211
00:46:16,079 --> 00:46:20,880
repetition of pulses and enables much

1212
00:46:18,400 --> 00:46:23,359
faster imaging, often completing a full

1213
00:46:20,880 --> 00:46:25,359
scan in less than a minute.

1214
00:46:23,359 --> 00:46:27,680
There are several variations of gradient

1215
00:46:25,359 --> 00:46:30,319
echo sequences including flash which

1216
00:46:27,680 --> 00:46:32,240
stands for fast low angle shot, FISP or

1217
00:46:30,319 --> 00:46:34,240
fast imaging with steady procession,

1218
00:46:32,240 --> 00:46:37,119
grass which uses gradient refocused

1219
00:46:34,240 --> 00:46:40,079
acquisition in the steady state and SSFP

1220
00:46:37,119 --> 00:46:41,839
or steady state free procession.

1221
00:46:40,079 --> 00:46:43,839
Each of these methods is built on the

1222
00:46:41,839 --> 00:46:46,079
same principle using small flip angles

1223
00:46:43,839 --> 00:46:48,000
and gradient reasing instead of a

1224
00:46:46,079 --> 00:46:49,599
180°ree pulse to achieve high-speed

1225
00:46:48,000 --> 00:46:52,319
imaging.

1226
00:46:49,599 --> 00:46:54,319
For any given repetition time TR, there

1227
00:46:52,319 --> 00:46:56,960
exists an optimal flip angle that

1228
00:46:54,319 --> 00:46:59,200
maximizes the signal to noise ratio.

1229
00:46:56,960 --> 00:47:01,920
This is known as the Ernst angle given

1230
00:46:59,200 --> 00:47:06,640
by the formula alpha Ernst equals cosine

1231
00:47:01,920 --> 00:47:08,640
inverse of E to the power of TR / T1.

1232
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:10,560
By choosing the Ernst angle, we balance

1233
00:47:08,640 --> 00:47:12,560
image contrast and signal strength for

1234
00:47:10,560 --> 00:47:15,280
efficient imaging.

1235
00:47:12,560 --> 00:47:17,200
So to summarize, spin echo sequences

1236
00:47:15,280 --> 00:47:19,839
give you excellent image quality, but

1237
00:47:17,200 --> 00:47:21,920
require long scan times, gradient echo

1238
00:47:19,839 --> 00:47:23,760
sacrifices some signal stability for

1239
00:47:21,920 --> 00:47:25,760
dramatically faster acquisition. And

1240
00:47:23,760 --> 00:47:28,240
together, these two techniques form the

1241
00:47:25,760 --> 00:47:32,680
foundation of nearly all MRI pulse

1242
00:47:28,240 --> 00:47:32,680
sequences used in practice today.

1243
00:47:34,000 --> 00:47:38,079
Now let's look at three-dimensional

1244
00:47:35,680 --> 00:47:40,240
gradient echo imaging. In this method,

1245
00:47:38,079 --> 00:47:42,160
we extend the basic idea of gradient

1246
00:47:40,240 --> 00:47:44,400
echo imaging into three spatial

1247
00:47:42,160 --> 00:47:46,480
dimensions by using two separate phase

1248
00:47:44,400 --> 00:47:48,640
encoding gradients and one frequency

1249
00:47:46,480 --> 00:47:50,560
encoding gradient.

1250
00:47:48,640 --> 00:47:52,400
Here's how it works. We begin with a

1251
00:47:50,560 --> 00:47:55,200
radio frequency pulse that flips the

1252
00:47:52,400 --> 00:47:58,319
magnetization vector m by a small angle

1253
00:47:55,200 --> 00:48:00,160
alpha much less than 90°. Then the

1254
00:47:58,319 --> 00:48:02,880
system applies the first phase encoding

1255
00:48:00,160 --> 00:48:05,040
gradient labeled gphase 1 followed by a

1256
00:48:02,880 --> 00:48:07,119
frequency encoding gradient labeled G

1257
00:48:05,040 --> 00:48:10,640
fick to it and finally a second phase

1258
00:48:07,119 --> 00:48:12,880
encoding gradient gphase 2. This

1259
00:48:10,640 --> 00:48:15,920
sequence allows us to encode spatial

1260
00:48:12,880 --> 00:48:18,240
information along all three axes x y and

1261
00:48:15,920 --> 00:48:20,560
zed. So the final data form a

1262
00:48:18,240 --> 00:48:22,559
three-dimensional forier transform.

1263
00:48:20,560 --> 00:48:24,480
Mathematically the reconstructed signal

1264
00:48:22,559 --> 00:48:28,960
can be expressed as a triple integral

1265
00:48:24,480 --> 00:48:33,119
over kx ya k i and kz. That is ro of x y

1266
00:48:28,960 --> 00:48:37,839
z equals the integral of s of kx k i kz

1267
00:48:33,119 --> 00:48:43,839
* e to the power of j 2 pi * kx + ky y +

1268
00:48:37,839 --> 00:48:46,000
kzz integrated over d kx dky and dkz.

1269
00:48:43,839 --> 00:48:48,000
Each phase encoding step changes the

1270
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:50,400
gradient strength slightly producing a

1271
00:48:48,000 --> 00:48:52,160
different kspace plane.

1272
00:48:50,400 --> 00:48:54,160
When we collect all these planes and

1273
00:48:52,160 --> 00:48:56,640
perform a three-dimensional inverse

1274
00:48:54,160 --> 00:48:58,880
forier transform, we obtain a volutric

1275
00:48:56,640 --> 00:49:00,720
image of the scanned region. Because the

1276
00:48:58,880 --> 00:49:03,119
signal is read out immediately after

1277
00:49:00,720 --> 00:49:05,680
excitation, it decays rather quickly due

1278
00:49:03,119 --> 00:49:07,599
to T2 star relaxation. However, this

1279
00:49:05,680 --> 00:49:09,839
approach allows for very rapid data

1280
00:49:07,599 --> 00:49:11,440
acquisition, enabling highresolution

1281
00:49:09,839 --> 00:49:13,920
three-dimensional imaging in a

1282
00:49:11,440 --> 00:49:15,760
relatively short time.

1283
00:49:13,920 --> 00:49:17,599
Compared with spin echo sequences, the

1284
00:49:15,760 --> 00:49:19,040
signal to noise ratio is somewhat lower

1285
00:49:17,599 --> 00:49:21,200
because gradient echo does not

1286
00:49:19,040 --> 00:49:23,359
compensate for field in homogeneities,

1287
00:49:21,200 --> 00:49:25,839
but the speed advantage is substantial.

1288
00:49:23,359 --> 00:49:27,760
Scans can be completed much faster. To

1289
00:49:25,839 --> 00:49:30,960
achieve this balance, we use a small

1290
00:49:27,760 --> 00:49:32,880
flip angle rather than a large one. If

1291
00:49:30,960 --> 00:49:35,359
the flip angle is too large, the

1292
00:49:32,880 --> 00:49:37,280
magnetization along the Z axis doesn't

1293
00:49:35,359 --> 00:49:39,359
have enough time to recover before the

1294
00:49:37,280 --> 00:49:42,640
next excitation, and the signal from

1295
00:49:39,359 --> 00:49:44,800
subsequent repetitions becomes weaker.

1296
00:49:42,640 --> 00:49:47,440
By using a smaller flip angle and a

1297
00:49:44,800 --> 00:49:49,520
short repetition time or TR, we can

1298
00:49:47,440 --> 00:49:51,440
repeatedly excite and read out signals

1299
00:49:49,520 --> 00:49:53,839
quickly, maintaining good image quality

1300
00:49:51,440 --> 00:49:55,920
while minimizing scan duration. In

1301
00:49:53,839 --> 00:49:57,920
short, three-dimensional gradient echo

1302
00:49:55,920 --> 00:49:59,599
imaging sacrifices a bit of signal

1303
00:49:57,920 --> 00:50:02,800
strength for a dramatic improvement in

1304
00:49:59,599 --> 00:50:05,119
imaging speed. It is particularly useful

1305
00:50:02,800 --> 00:50:07,520
for volutric scans of the brain, heart,

1306
00:50:05,119 --> 00:50:09,760
and other organs where time is critical.

1307
00:50:07,520 --> 00:50:11,839
And if we push the concept even further,

1308
00:50:09,760 --> 00:50:14,000
we arrive at echoplaner imaging which

1309
00:50:11,839 --> 00:50:16,160
can acquire an entire image or even a

1310
00:50:14,000 --> 00:50:17,839
full volume in just one or a few rapid

1311
00:50:16,160 --> 00:50:20,160
readouts.

1312
00:50:17,839 --> 00:50:22,880
Echoplaner imaging is the foundation for

1313
00:50:20,160 --> 00:50:25,040
realtime MRI and functional MRI studies

1314
00:50:22,880 --> 00:50:28,359
where both speed and temporal resolution

1315
00:50:25,040 --> 00:50:28,359
are essential.

1316
00:50:29,839 --> 00:50:34,240
Now we come to one of the fastest MRI

1317
00:50:31,920 --> 00:50:36,640
imaging techniques, echoplaner imaging

1318
00:50:34,240 --> 00:50:39,280
or EPI. In this sequence, the

1319
00:50:36,640 --> 00:50:42,160
magnetization vector M is first flipped

1320
00:50:39,280 --> 00:50:44,240
into the XY plane using a 90deree radio

1321
00:50:42,160 --> 00:50:46,160
frequency pulse.

1322
00:50:44,240 --> 00:50:48,559
Next, slice selection is performed to

1323
00:50:46,160 --> 00:50:51,599
isolate a specific plane. For example,

1324
00:50:48,559 --> 00:50:53,280
the XY plane of the imaging region. Once

1325
00:50:51,599 --> 00:50:55,119
the slice is selected, the imaging

1326
00:50:53,280 --> 00:50:57,599
process begins with a combination of

1327
00:50:55,119 --> 00:50:59,359
phase encoding and frequency encoding

1328
00:50:57,599 --> 00:51:01,440
that alternate in an oscillating

1329
00:50:59,359 --> 00:51:03,599
pattern. This means that the frequency

1330
00:51:01,440 --> 00:51:05,359
encoding gradient repeatedly switches

1331
00:51:03,599 --> 00:51:07,920
direction. first positive, then

1332
00:51:05,359 --> 00:51:10,640
negative, then positive again while data

1333
00:51:07,920 --> 00:51:12,559
are continuously acquired.

1334
00:51:10,640 --> 00:51:14,640
As a result, the signal alternates

1335
00:51:12,559 --> 00:51:16,880
between positive and negative readouts

1336
00:51:14,640 --> 00:51:19,280
forming a continuous train of echoes.

1337
00:51:16,880 --> 00:51:21,760
Each echo corresponds to one horizontal

1338
00:51:19,280 --> 00:51:24,480
line in kspace and the entire pattern

1339
00:51:21,760 --> 00:51:26,880
forms a zigzag trajectory across kspace

1340
00:51:24,480 --> 00:51:28,960
as shown on the right side of the slide.

1341
00:51:26,880 --> 00:51:31,040
Let's interpret this step by step. Slice

1342
00:51:28,960 --> 00:51:33,760
selection determines the imaging plane,

1343
00:51:31,040 --> 00:51:36,160
typically the XY plane. Phase encoding

1344
00:51:33,760 --> 00:51:38,640
moves the position in Kspace vertically

1345
00:51:36,160 --> 00:51:41,599
that is along the KI direction shifting

1346
00:51:38,640 --> 00:51:43,520
the data line by line.

1347
00:51:41,599 --> 00:51:46,079
Frequency encoding determines the

1348
00:51:43,520 --> 00:51:48,400
horizontal readout or the kx direction

1349
00:51:46,079 --> 00:51:50,319
as the data are collected. Each line of

1350
00:51:48,400 --> 00:51:51,839
kspace is traversed in alternating

1351
00:51:50,319 --> 00:51:53,680
directions. The first line moves from

1352
00:51:51,839 --> 00:51:55,760
left to right, the next line from right

1353
00:51:53,680 --> 00:51:57,839
to left and so on. This continuous

1354
00:51:55,760 --> 00:52:00,160
acquisition pattern allows us to collect

1355
00:51:57,839 --> 00:52:02,800
a full image or at least a major portion

1356
00:52:00,160 --> 00:52:05,119
of it in a single shot.

1357
00:52:02,800 --> 00:52:07,280
However, as data acquisition progresses,

1358
00:52:05,119 --> 00:52:09,839
the signal amplitude gradually decreases

1359
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:12,160
due to T2 star decay, which limits how

1360
00:52:09,839 --> 00:52:14,160
long useful data can be collected.

1361
00:52:12,160 --> 00:52:16,480
Because of this decay, echoplaner

1362
00:52:14,160 --> 00:52:19,040
imaging may sometimes cover only part of

1363
00:52:16,480 --> 00:52:21,119
kspace during one acquisition, requiring

1364
00:52:19,040 --> 00:52:22,720
multiple passes to fill in the remaining

1365
00:52:21,119 --> 00:52:24,720
data.

1366
00:52:22,720 --> 00:52:26,559
Even with that limitation, EPI is

1367
00:52:24,720 --> 00:52:29,119
dramatically faster than conventional

1368
00:52:26,559 --> 00:52:31,440
three-dimensional gradient echo imaging.

1369
00:52:29,119 --> 00:52:33,920
In fact, it can complete a full image in

1370
00:52:31,440 --> 00:52:36,240
just a fraction of a second.

1371
00:52:33,920 --> 00:52:38,880
That's why echoplaner imaging has become

1372
00:52:36,240 --> 00:52:41,359
the foundation for realtime MRI and

1373
00:52:38,880 --> 00:52:43,280
functional MRI where rapid temporal

1374
00:52:41,359 --> 00:52:45,920
sampling is more important than perfect

1375
00:52:43,280 --> 00:52:47,920
image contrast. And when we want to go

1376
00:52:45,920 --> 00:52:50,400
even faster, we can move beyond

1377
00:52:47,920 --> 00:52:52,800
echoplaner imaging to spiral imaging,

1378
00:52:50,400 --> 00:52:54,559
which traces kspace in smooth circular

1379
00:52:52,800 --> 00:52:58,280
trajectories, further reducing

1380
00:52:54,559 --> 00:52:58,280
acquisition time.

1381
00:52:59,440 --> 00:53:03,760
Now let's move on to spiral imaging. A

1382
00:53:02,000 --> 00:53:05,920
powerful and elegant approach that

1383
00:53:03,760 --> 00:53:09,280
enables even faster data acquisition

1384
00:53:05,920 --> 00:53:11,280
than echoplaner imaging. In spiral

1385
00:53:09,280 --> 00:53:13,920
imaging, there is no separate phase

1386
00:53:11,280 --> 00:53:16,640
encoding step. Instead, both gradient

1387
00:53:13,920 --> 00:53:19,040
fields gx and gy continuously change

1388
00:53:16,640 --> 00:53:20,400
their amplitudes over time. The relative

1389
00:53:19,040 --> 00:53:22,400
strength of these two gradients

1390
00:53:20,400 --> 00:53:25,200
determines the instantaneous slope of

1391
00:53:22,400 --> 00:53:27,200
the trajectory in kspace.

1392
00:53:25,200 --> 00:53:29,280
At the very beginning, the system starts

1393
00:53:27,200 --> 00:53:31,280
from the origin of k space, meaning the

1394
00:53:29,280 --> 00:53:33,839
initial point of the phase vector is

1395
00:53:31,280 --> 00:53:36,800
zero. As time progresses, the amplitudes

1396
00:53:33,839 --> 00:53:38,880
of gx and gy vary smoothly, causing the

1397
00:53:36,800 --> 00:53:41,760
trajectory to move outward in a spiral

1398
00:53:38,880 --> 00:53:43,359
pattern. The direction of this spiral is

1399
00:53:41,760 --> 00:53:45,119
controlled by the relative phase and

1400
00:53:43,359 --> 00:53:47,359
amplitude ratio between the two

1401
00:53:45,119 --> 00:53:49,359
gradients. Initially, the trajectory

1402
00:53:47,359 --> 00:53:51,280
moves along a specific direction for a

1403
00:53:49,359 --> 00:53:53,680
short distance.

1404
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:55,599
Then as the relative gradient amplitudes

1405
00:53:53,680 --> 00:53:58,079
change, the slope and direction of the

1406
00:53:55,599 --> 00:54:00,240
path gradually rotate. This continuous

1407
00:53:58,079 --> 00:54:02,720
change allows the kspace trajectory to

1408
00:54:00,240 --> 00:54:04,559
trace a smooth spiral curve expanding

1409
00:54:02,720 --> 00:54:07,280
outward from the center as higher

1410
00:54:04,559 --> 00:54:08,960
spatial frequencies are collected.

1411
00:54:07,280 --> 00:54:11,440
The advantage of this approach is that

1412
00:54:08,960 --> 00:54:13,359
it provides continuous control over both

1413
00:54:11,440 --> 00:54:15,680
gradients allowing highly efficient

1414
00:54:13,359 --> 00:54:17,200
coverage of kspace. Depending on the

1415
00:54:15,680 --> 00:54:18,880
design, the spiral can move from the

1416
00:54:17,200 --> 00:54:21,040
center outward, from the periphery

1417
00:54:18,880 --> 00:54:23,440
inward, or even use multiple interleaf

1418
00:54:21,040 --> 00:54:25,839
spirals that fill KS space more densely

1419
00:54:23,440 --> 00:54:27,920
and reduce artifacts. Because of this

1420
00:54:25,839 --> 00:54:29,839
flexibility, spiral imaging offers

1421
00:54:27,920 --> 00:54:32,400
excellent speed while still maintaining

1422
00:54:29,839 --> 00:54:34,480
high spatial resolution.

1423
00:54:32,400 --> 00:54:36,559
It captures low frequency components

1424
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:38,400
first, which contain the overall image

1425
00:54:36,559 --> 00:54:40,400
contrast, and then gradually collects

1426
00:54:38,400 --> 00:54:43,040
higher frequency components that define

1427
00:54:40,400 --> 00:54:45,040
fine structural detail.

1428
00:54:43,040 --> 00:54:47,359
After data acquisition, the spiral

1429
00:54:45,040 --> 00:54:49,280
kspace data must be interpolated onto a

1430
00:54:47,359 --> 00:54:51,440
rectangular grid before applying the

1431
00:54:49,280 --> 00:54:54,319
two-dimensional inverse forier transform

1432
00:54:51,440 --> 00:54:57,200
to reconstruct the image. This

1433
00:54:54,319 --> 00:54:59,200
reconstruction yields T1 weighted, T2

1434
00:54:57,200 --> 00:55:01,040
weighted or proton density weighted

1435
00:54:59,200 --> 00:55:03,520
images depending on how the pulse

1436
00:55:01,040 --> 00:55:06,960
sequence parameters such as flip angle

1437
00:55:03,520 --> 00:55:08,800
TE and TR are chosen.

1438
00:55:06,960 --> 00:55:11,359
Understanding how data are collected in

1439
00:55:08,800 --> 00:55:13,359
Kspace is crucial. If you fully grasp

1440
00:55:11,359 --> 00:55:16,160
how the gradients define the kspace

1441
00:55:13,359 --> 00:55:19,200
trajectory, whether linear, zigzag or

1442
00:55:16,160 --> 00:55:21,760
spiral, the entire process of MRI image

1443
00:55:19,200 --> 00:55:25,599
formation becomes intuitive. Otherwise,

1444
00:55:21,760 --> 00:55:27,760
it can seem abstract or confusing.

1445
00:55:25,599 --> 00:55:29,680
Finally, these flexible gradient-based

1446
00:55:27,760 --> 00:55:32,559
trajectories also open the door to

1447
00:55:29,680 --> 00:55:35,280
specialized MRI pulse sequences. For

1448
00:55:32,559 --> 00:55:37,040
example, in MR angography, we can tailor

1449
00:55:35,280 --> 00:55:38,880
the sequence to highlight blood flow,

1450
00:55:37,040 --> 00:55:41,520
just as CT angography does for

1451
00:55:38,880 --> 00:55:43,680
X-raybased imaging. By manipulating the

1452
00:55:41,520 --> 00:55:46,079
gradient and timing parameters, we can

1453
00:55:43,680 --> 00:55:48,079
emphasize vascular structures and obtain

1454
00:55:46,079 --> 00:55:50,480
clear highresolution images of the

1455
00:55:48,079 --> 00:55:52,960
circulatory system.

1456
00:55:50,480 --> 00:55:55,119
So spiral imaging not only accelerates

1457
00:55:52,960 --> 00:55:56,960
acquisition but also serves as a bridge

1458
00:55:55,119 --> 00:55:58,960
to more advanced and application

1459
00:55:56,960 --> 00:56:01,200
specific MRI techniques such as

1460
00:55:58,960 --> 00:56:04,760
angography, diffusion imaging and

1461
00:56:01,200 --> 00:56:04,760
functional MRI.

1462
00:56:06,160 --> 00:56:10,240
Let's now talk about time offlight

1463
00:56:07,839 --> 00:56:11,760
imaging or TOF which is a fundamental

1464
00:56:10,240 --> 00:56:14,720
technique in magnetic resonance

1465
00:56:11,760 --> 00:56:16,640
angography or MRA. In a standard CT

1466
00:56:14,720 --> 00:56:19,680
image, blood vessels and soft tissues

1467
00:56:16,640 --> 00:56:21,119
often appear very similar in density.

1468
00:56:19,680 --> 00:56:23,040
As a result, it's difficult to

1469
00:56:21,119 --> 00:56:25,200
distinguish the vascule. You can't

1470
00:56:23,040 --> 00:56:27,599
easily tell whether a vessel is open,

1471
00:56:25,200 --> 00:56:30,000
narrowed, or completely blocked. To

1472
00:56:27,599 --> 00:56:32,640
solve this problem in CT, we use a

1473
00:56:30,000 --> 00:56:34,799
contrast agent, typically iodine, a

1474
00:56:32,640 --> 00:56:37,520
liquid metal with a very high linear

1475
00:56:34,799 --> 00:56:39,440
attenuation coefficient.

1476
00:56:37,520 --> 00:56:41,680
When injected into the bloodstream,

1477
00:56:39,440 --> 00:56:43,599
iodine increases the attenuation of

1478
00:56:41,680 --> 00:56:45,040
X-rays in the blood, making vessels

1479
00:56:43,599 --> 00:56:47,440
appear much brighter than the

1480
00:56:45,040 --> 00:56:49,359
surrounding soft tissue.

1481
00:56:47,440 --> 00:56:51,839
If a vessel that should be wide suddenly

1482
00:56:49,359 --> 00:56:53,920
appears narrow or faint in the CT image

1483
00:56:51,839 --> 00:56:55,760
that suggests a vascular blockage which

1484
00:56:53,920 --> 00:56:58,079
could require stenting or further

1485
00:56:55,760 --> 00:57:00,319
cardiac intervention.

1486
00:56:58,079 --> 00:57:03,440
This is the basic principle behind CT

1487
00:57:00,319 --> 00:57:05,200
angography. Now for MRI angography the

1488
00:57:03,440 --> 00:57:07,040
principle is different but the goal is

1489
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:08,720
the same to make the blood vessels

1490
00:57:07,040 --> 00:57:11,280
appear distinct from the surrounding

1491
00:57:08,720 --> 00:57:13,680
tissue. Here's how it works. We first

1492
00:57:11,280 --> 00:57:16,559
select a single imaging slice and then

1493
00:57:13,680 --> 00:57:19,359
apply a radio frequency pulse typically

1494
00:57:16,559 --> 00:57:21,599
using an echoplaner imaging sequence to

1495
00:57:19,359 --> 00:57:24,640
flip the spins those small magnetic

1496
00:57:21,599 --> 00:57:26,720
dipoles within that slice.

1497
00:57:24,640 --> 00:57:28,480
At that instant both the blood within

1498
00:57:26,720 --> 00:57:30,240
the vessel and the stationary tissue

1499
00:57:28,480 --> 00:57:32,079
generate signals that are quite similar.

1500
00:57:30,240 --> 00:57:34,559
So initially the vessel is not clearly

1501
00:57:32,079 --> 00:57:36,799
visible. But if we wait a little longer

1502
00:57:34,559 --> 00:57:38,720
something interesting happens. The blood

1503
00:57:36,799 --> 00:57:40,799
that was originally in the imaging slice

1504
00:57:38,720 --> 00:57:43,119
flows out of the plane and new blood

1505
00:57:40,799 --> 00:57:44,880
containing unflipped spins flows in from

1506
00:57:43,119 --> 00:57:46,559
outside.

1507
00:57:44,880 --> 00:57:48,640
This is what we call the through plane

1508
00:57:46,559 --> 00:57:50,400
mechanism. Because the newly arrived

1509
00:57:48,640 --> 00:57:52,480
blood has not been excited by the RF

1510
00:57:50,400 --> 00:57:54,160
pulse, it produces no signal in that

1511
00:57:52,480 --> 00:57:56,160
moment. Meanwhile, the surrounding

1512
00:57:54,160 --> 00:57:58,160
stationary tissue continues to emit its

1513
00:57:56,160 --> 00:58:00,400
signal which gradually decays but does

1514
00:57:58,160 --> 00:58:02,319
not vanish immediately.

1515
00:58:00,400 --> 00:58:04,160
As a result, when we collect the data

1516
00:58:02,319 --> 00:58:05,839
after a short delay, the moving blood

1517
00:58:04,160 --> 00:58:08,240
appears bright while the stationary

1518
00:58:05,839 --> 00:58:09,839
tissue looks relatively dark. This

1519
00:58:08,240 --> 00:58:12,559
contrast difference allows us to

1520
00:58:09,839 --> 00:58:14,240
visualize the vascule clearly. The time

1521
00:58:12,559 --> 00:58:15,920
of flight effect is strongest when the

1522
00:58:14,240 --> 00:58:18,000
blood flow is perpendicular to the

1523
00:58:15,920 --> 00:58:19,200
imaging slice, that is when blood moves

1524
00:58:18,000 --> 00:58:21,040
directly through the plane of

1525
00:58:19,200 --> 00:58:22,720
excitation.

1526
00:58:21,040 --> 00:58:24,640
If the vessel runs within the plane of

1527
00:58:22,720 --> 00:58:26,559
the slice, then the same spins remain in

1528
00:58:24,640 --> 00:58:29,520
the imaging region and the through plane

1529
00:58:26,559 --> 00:58:32,799
mechanism does not work effectively.

1530
00:58:29,520 --> 00:58:35,839
So to summarize in CT angography iodine

1531
00:58:32,799 --> 00:58:37,920
contrast enhances X-ray attenuation in

1532
00:58:35,839 --> 00:58:40,000
Msar angography the motion of blood

1533
00:58:37,920 --> 00:58:41,839
itself provides the contrast through the

1534
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:43,839
time of flight effect and the through

1535
00:58:41,839 --> 00:58:46,000
plane mechanism.

1536
00:58:43,839 --> 00:58:48,720
This principle forms the foundation of

1537
00:58:46,000 --> 00:58:50,480
non-contrast MR angography allowing

1538
00:58:48,720 --> 00:58:55,319
clear visualization of blood flow

1539
00:58:50,480 --> 00:58:55,319
without any injected contrast agents.

1540
00:58:56,799 --> 00:59:00,319
Now let's move on to phase contrast

1541
00:58:58,480 --> 00:59:02,960
imaging which is another powerful method

1542
00:59:00,319 --> 00:59:05,119
used in MR angography. Unlike the

1543
00:59:02,960 --> 00:59:06,799
timeofflight technique that works mainly

1544
00:59:05,119 --> 00:59:09,119
through the throughplane mechanism,

1545
00:59:06,799 --> 00:59:11,200
phase contrast imaging is an inplane

1546
00:59:09,119 --> 00:59:13,119
mechanism, meaning it can detect blood

1547
00:59:11,200 --> 00:59:15,920
flow in any direction, not just

1548
00:59:13,119 --> 00:59:17,920
perpendicular to the slice.

1549
00:59:15,920 --> 00:59:20,640
The basic idea is similar to what we've

1550
00:59:17,920 --> 00:59:22,319
seen in echoplaner imaging. We start

1551
00:59:20,640 --> 00:59:25,280
with an alpha pulse that flips the

1552
00:59:22,319 --> 00:59:27,520
magnetization vector M by an angle alpha

1553
00:59:25,280 --> 00:59:29,440
for slice selection.

1554
00:59:27,520 --> 00:59:31,520
All the spins within the selected slice

1555
00:59:29,440 --> 00:59:33,920
then work together to produce a coherent

1556
00:59:31,520 --> 00:59:35,520
signal. As usual, frequency encoding and

1557
00:59:33,920 --> 00:59:38,640
phase encoding are applied to collect

1558
00:59:35,520 --> 00:59:40,960
the forier information from that slice.

1559
00:59:38,640 --> 00:59:42,880
However, in a normal forier based MRI

1560
00:59:40,960 --> 00:59:45,920
scan, the pixel intensities for blood

1561
00:59:42,880 --> 00:59:48,400
and stationary tissue in terms of T1, T2

1562
00:59:45,920 --> 00:59:50,160
or proton density often appear very

1563
00:59:48,400 --> 00:59:52,079
similar. This makes it difficult to

1564
00:59:50,160 --> 00:59:55,200
distinguish flowing blood from static

1565
00:59:52,079 --> 00:59:57,440
tissue. To separate them, we introduce a

1566
00:59:55,200 --> 00:59:59,599
pair of bipolar gradient pulses, one

1567
00:59:57,440 --> 01:00:01,760
positive and one negative, before data

1568
00:59:59,599 --> 01:00:04,160
acquisition. The first or positive

1569
01:00:01,760 --> 01:00:06,240
gradient pulse adds a phase shift to all

1570
01:00:04,160 --> 01:00:08,240
spins, and the second or negative

1571
01:00:06,240 --> 01:00:10,640
gradient pulse adds an equal but

1572
01:00:08,240 --> 01:00:12,640
opposite phase shift.

1573
01:00:10,640 --> 01:00:14,480
For stationary spins, such as those in

1574
01:00:12,640 --> 01:00:16,720
soft tissue, these two effects cancel

1575
01:00:14,480 --> 01:00:18,559
perfectly, leaving no net phase change.

1576
01:00:16,720 --> 01:00:20,079
But if the spins are moving as in

1577
01:00:18,559 --> 01:00:22,480
flowing blood, something different

1578
01:00:20,079 --> 01:00:24,480
happens between the two gradient pulses.

1579
01:00:22,480 --> 01:00:26,240
The blood cells physically move to a new

1580
01:00:24,480 --> 01:00:29,280
position. So they experience slightly

1581
01:00:26,240 --> 01:00:30,960
different local magnetic fields. As a

1582
01:00:29,280 --> 01:00:33,200
result, the positive and negative

1583
01:00:30,960 --> 01:00:35,119
gradients do not cancel out completely.

1584
01:00:33,200 --> 01:00:37,280
The motion introduces an extra phase

1585
01:00:35,119 --> 01:00:38,880
term that accumulates over time and this

1586
01:00:37,280 --> 01:00:40,960
additional phase is directly

1587
01:00:38,880 --> 01:00:43,119
proportional to the velocity of the

1588
01:00:40,960 --> 01:00:44,559
moving spins. By measuring the

1589
01:00:43,119 --> 01:00:46,960
difference in phase between two

1590
01:00:44,559 --> 01:00:49,200
acquisitions, one with and one without

1591
01:00:46,960 --> 01:00:50,880
motion encoding, we can isolate the

1592
01:00:49,200 --> 01:00:54,160
component of the signal that depends

1593
01:00:50,880 --> 01:00:56,160
purely on blood flow. This produces a

1594
01:00:54,160 --> 01:00:58,640
phase contrast image where bright

1595
01:00:56,160 --> 01:01:01,839
regions correspond to areas of flow and

1596
01:00:58,640 --> 01:01:04,079
darker regions to stationary tissue.

1597
01:01:01,839 --> 01:01:06,720
In mathematical form, this accumulated

1598
01:01:04,079 --> 01:01:08,799
phase fi is given by an integral of the

1599
01:01:06,720 --> 01:01:12,559
gradient over time. phi equals the

1600
01:01:08,799 --> 01:01:15,680
integral from 0 to tow / 2 of gamma * gx

1601
01:01:12,559 --> 01:01:21,359
* x of t dt plus the integral from toao

1602
01:01:15,680 --> 01:01:23,200
over 2 to gamma * gx * x of t dt.

1603
01:01:21,359 --> 01:01:25,119
The net phase difference reflects the

1604
01:01:23,200 --> 01:01:27,280
motion of the spins under the bipolar

1605
01:01:25,119 --> 01:01:29,760
gradients. Conceptually, you can think

1606
01:01:27,280 --> 01:01:31,119
of time offlight imaging as a passive

1607
01:01:29,760 --> 01:01:33,680
effect where the signal difference

1608
01:01:31,119 --> 01:01:36,880
arises simply because new unflipped

1609
01:01:33,680 --> 01:01:38,880
blood enters the slice. In contrast,

1610
01:01:36,880 --> 01:01:41,280
phase contrast imaging is an active

1611
01:01:38,880 --> 01:01:43,040
mechanism where we intentionally encode

1612
01:01:41,280 --> 01:01:45,520
blood motion into the phase of the

1613
01:01:43,040 --> 01:01:47,680
signal. And unlike X-ray phase contrast

1614
01:01:45,520 --> 01:01:50,400
imaging, which measures refractive index

1615
01:01:47,680 --> 01:01:52,559
variations, MRI phase contrast imaging

1616
01:01:50,400 --> 01:01:55,599
detects the phase shift of the magnetic

1617
01:01:52,559 --> 01:01:56,960
vector caused by moving spins.

1618
01:01:55,599 --> 01:01:59,280
It doesn't matter whether the blood

1619
01:01:56,960 --> 01:02:01,040
flows up, down, or sideways. As long as

1620
01:01:59,280 --> 01:02:03,200
the spins move through regions with

1621
01:02:01,040 --> 01:02:05,119
slightly different magnetic fields, the

1622
01:02:03,200 --> 01:02:07,520
phase difference can be detected and

1623
01:02:05,119 --> 01:02:09,440
visualized. By exploiting these motion

1624
01:02:07,520 --> 01:02:12,160
induced phase shifts, we can generate

1625
01:02:09,440 --> 01:02:15,520
clear vascular images and even quantify

1626
01:02:12,160 --> 01:02:17,599
blood velocity or flow rate. Together

1627
01:02:15,520 --> 01:02:19,680
with time offlight imaging, phase

1628
01:02:17,599 --> 01:02:22,000
contrast imaging provides a complete

1629
01:02:19,680 --> 01:02:24,000
toolkit for visualizing the circulatory

1630
01:02:22,000 --> 01:02:27,480
system without the need for injected

1631
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:27,480
contrast agents.

1632
01:02:28,960 --> 01:02:33,680
Now let's talk about diffusion weighted

1633
01:02:30,960 --> 01:02:35,440
imaging or DWI for short. Remember our

1634
01:02:33,680 --> 01:02:37,280
body is made up mostly of water and

1635
01:02:35,440 --> 01:02:39,200
these water molecules are constantly

1636
01:02:37,280 --> 01:02:41,440
moving around.

1637
01:02:39,200 --> 01:02:43,520
This motion or diffusion becomes

1638
01:02:41,440 --> 01:02:45,520
physiologically and pathologically

1639
01:02:43,520 --> 01:02:47,520
important especially when something goes

1640
01:02:45,520 --> 01:02:49,680
wrong at the cellular level. For

1641
01:02:47,520 --> 01:02:51,839
example, when a cell membrane becomes

1642
01:02:49,680 --> 01:02:53,440
swollen or damaged, water can leak out

1643
01:02:51,839 --> 01:02:55,599
of the cell altering the normal

1644
01:02:53,440 --> 01:02:57,200
diffusion and perusion patterns in that

1645
01:02:55,599 --> 01:02:59,599
tissue.

1646
