2640
01:40:57,840 --> 01:41:01,840
where complimentary information from

2641
01:40:59,679 --> 01:41:03,840
different imaging mechanisms is acquired

2642
01:41:01,840 --> 01:41:07,719
concurrently for the most comprehensive

2643
01:41:03,840 --> 01:41:07,719
view of the human body.

2644
01:41:09,040 --> 01:41:12,800
Now let's look at the motivation for

2645
01:41:10,960 --> 01:41:15,840
simultaneous emission transmission

2646
01:41:12,800 --> 01:41:17,679
tomography or sets. This idea comes from

2647
01:41:15,840 --> 01:41:20,320
a concept called polarized nuclear

2648
01:41:17,679 --> 01:41:24,080
imaging abbreviated as PNI which builds

2649
01:41:20,320 --> 01:41:25,760
on the magnetic resonance MR framework.

2650
01:41:24,080 --> 01:41:28,080
In conventional nuclear imaging,

2651
01:41:25,760 --> 01:41:30,159
gammaray photons are emitted randomly in

2652
01:41:28,080 --> 01:41:31,920
all directions. However, with a

2653
01:41:30,159 --> 01:41:34,159
polarized radio tracer, the emission

2654
01:41:31,920 --> 01:41:36,000
becomes directionally dependent. That

2655
01:41:34,159 --> 01:41:37,760
means the gamma rays are no longer

2656
01:41:36,000 --> 01:41:40,800
isotropic. They are preferentially

2657
01:41:37,760 --> 01:41:43,040
emitted along specific directions.

2658
01:41:40,800 --> 01:41:45,280
As shown in the figure, when nuclei are

2659
01:41:43,040 --> 01:41:47,040
fully polarized along a magnetic field,

2660
01:41:45,280 --> 01:41:49,119
the emitted gamma rays have a strong

2661
01:41:47,040 --> 01:41:52,560
preference for the horizontal direction

2662
01:41:49,119 --> 01:41:55,199
and almost none are emitted vertically.

2663
01:41:52,560 --> 01:41:57,920
By using RF pulses and magnetic field

2664
01:41:55,199 --> 01:42:00,239
manipulations similar to those in MRI,

2665
01:41:57,920 --> 01:42:02,080
we can flip this polarization vector,

2666
01:42:00,239 --> 01:42:04,639
sometimes called the M vector, to

2667
01:42:02,080 --> 01:42:06,480
control the emission direction.

2668
01:42:04,639 --> 01:42:08,560
In other words, by flipping the spins

2669
01:42:06,480 --> 01:42:10,800
with an RF pulse, we can switch from

2670
01:42:08,560 --> 01:42:12,320
collecting gamma rays along the XY plane

2671
01:42:10,800 --> 01:42:14,639
to collecting them along the Z

2672
01:42:12,320 --> 01:42:16,880
direction. This enables us to encode

2673
01:42:14,639 --> 01:42:19,280
spatial information into the atomic spin

2674
01:42:16,880 --> 01:42:21,040
orientation, essentially merging nuclear

2675
01:42:19,280 --> 01:42:22,960
imaging and magnetic resonance

2676
01:42:21,040 --> 01:42:25,040
mechanisms.

2677
01:42:22,960 --> 01:42:27,119
The result is that polarized nuclear

2678
01:42:25,040 --> 01:42:29,520
imaging provides much higher sensitivity

2679
01:42:27,119 --> 01:42:31,520
than conventional MRI since the signal

2680
01:42:29,520 --> 01:42:33,360
is derived directly from gamma emission

2681
01:42:31,520 --> 01:42:35,520
rather than from weak magnetic

2682
01:42:33,360 --> 01:42:37,119
resonance. The only drawback is that it

2683
01:42:35,520 --> 01:42:40,480
does not account for attenuation which

2684
01:42:37,119 --> 01:42:42,400
is usually handled by CT. Our recent

2685
01:42:40,480 --> 01:42:44,639
paper builds on this idea, showing that

2686
01:42:42,400 --> 01:42:46,880
by using a polarized tracer, we can

2687
01:42:44,639 --> 01:42:48,719
reconstruct both the radioactive tracer

2688
01:42:46,880 --> 01:42:50,639
concentration and the attenuation

2689
01:42:48,719 --> 01:42:53,520
background simultaneously, combining

2690
01:42:50,639 --> 01:42:57,560
what MRI and CT each do best into a

2691
01:42:53,520 --> 01:42:57,560
single integrated framework.

2692
01:42:58,880 --> 01:43:02,560
With this polarized nuclear imaging

2693
01:43:00,719 --> 01:43:04,080
framework, we can perform both emission

2694
01:43:02,560 --> 01:43:06,480
and transmission tomography

2695
01:43:04,080 --> 01:43:08,400
simultaneously. However, to make this

2696
01:43:06,480 --> 01:43:11,119
practical, we need suitable polarized

2697
01:43:08,400 --> 01:43:13,119
radioisotopes or traces. This table

2698
01:43:11,119 --> 01:43:15,679
lists a few existing candidates,

2699
01:43:13,119 --> 01:43:17,679
isotopes of xenon and krypton that can

2700
01:43:15,679 --> 01:43:19,679
in principle be polarized and used for

2701
01:43:17,679 --> 01:43:22,719
such imaging.

2702
01:43:19,679 --> 01:43:25,199
For example, xenon 131m has a half-life

2703
01:43:22,719 --> 01:43:28,880
of about 12 days and a gamma emission

2704
01:43:25,199 --> 01:43:32,000
energy around 164 kilo electron volts.

2705
01:43:28,880 --> 01:43:34,320
Xenon 127 decays faster with a halflife

2706
01:43:32,000 --> 01:43:39,199
of 69 seconds emitting gamma rays

2707
01:43:34,320 --> 01:43:41,840
between 125 and 170 kilo electron volts

2708
01:43:39,199 --> 01:43:44,560
and krypton 79 has a halflife of about

2709
01:43:41,840 --> 01:43:47,119
50 seconds with gamma emission near 130

2710
01:43:44,560 --> 01:43:49,280
kilo electron volts.

2711
01:43:47,119 --> 01:43:51,360
You can see the challenge here. Ideally

2712
01:43:49,280 --> 01:43:53,600
a good clinical tracer should have a

2713
01:43:51,360 --> 01:43:55,760
halflife around 30 minutes. Long enough

2714
01:43:53,600 --> 01:43:58,080
for preparation and imaging but short

2715
01:43:55,760 --> 01:44:00,080
enough to minimize radiation dose.

2716
01:43:58,080 --> 01:44:02,159
Unfortunately, none of these isotopes

2717
01:44:00,080 --> 01:44:04,400
fit that range. They are either too long

2718
01:44:02,159 --> 01:44:05,840
or too short.

2719
01:44:04,400 --> 01:44:07,760
That's why our team has been

2720
01:44:05,840 --> 01:44:10,560
collaborating with chemists and nuclear

2721
01:44:07,760 --> 01:44:12,880
physicists to identify or synthesize new

2722
01:44:10,560 --> 01:44:15,119
polarizable traces with more suitable

2723
01:44:12,880 --> 01:44:17,119
half- livives and emission properties.

2724
01:44:15,119 --> 01:44:18,639
If such traces can be developed, they

2725
01:44:17,119 --> 01:44:21,119
would make simultaneous emission

2726
01:44:18,639 --> 01:44:25,719
transmission tomography a realistic and

2727
01:44:21,119 --> 01:44:25,719
powerful imaging tool for the future.

2728
01:44:26,880 --> 01:44:31,360
Now once we identify a suitable radio

2729
01:44:29,119 --> 01:44:33,679
tracer, the next step is to find a cost

2730
01:44:31,360 --> 01:44:35,679
effective way to polarize it so that we

2731
01:44:33,679 --> 01:44:37,360
can actually use it in imaging. This

2732
01:44:35,679 --> 01:44:41,199
figure shows one of the most common

2733
01:44:37,360 --> 01:44:42,880
methods. It's called optical pumping.

2734
01:44:41,199 --> 01:44:45,199
Here you see a glass cell. We call it

2735
01:44:42,880 --> 01:44:47,280
